WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.060
Resource safety starts with ownership.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:13.800
The component that creates a resource, opens a connection, receives a stream, rents a buffer, or takes responsibility for an object needs to know whether it owns that resource.

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:19.340
Ownership decides which code releases the resource and how long the resource should remain available.

4
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.100
In C#, IDisposable provides a standard pattern for deterministic cleanup.

5
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:37.560
It is used by many types that wrap unmanaged handles, file resources, network resources, timers, transactions, pooled values, or other objects that should be released before garbage collection happens.

6
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:43.660
Disposal is not only about memory; it is also about external capacity and predictable release.

7
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:50.900
The using statement and using declaration help ensure disposal occurs when control leaves a scope, including when exceptions occur.

8
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.400
That makes cleanup visible in the code and reduces the chance that error paths leave resources behind.

9
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:06.300
The pattern is especially useful for short-lived streams, commands, readers, transactions, timers, and other scoped objects.

10
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:09.940
Disposal order matters when resources depend on each other.

11
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.280
A stream wrapper may need to flush before its underlying stream is closed.

12
00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.660
A reader may depend on a command or connection.

13
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:23.120
A transaction may need a clear commit or rollback decision before the connection returns to a pool.

14
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.740
Good code makes those relationships explicit rather than relying on incidental order.

15
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.460
Resource management also means avoiding premature disposal.

16
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.520
A method should not dispose an object it does not own while another component still uses it.

17
00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:45.300
Passing disposable objects across layers requires agreement about ownership transfer, shared use, and release responsibility.

18
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:51.800
Static fields and long-lived singletons that hold disposables deserve deliberate lifetime review.

19
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.440
Garbage collection does not remove the need for this review.

20
00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:06.500
The runtime can reclaim managed memory eventually, but it does not promise immediate release of file handles, sockets, external capacity, locks, or service-side state.

21
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:11.960
Deterministic cleanup is the safer baseline when the resource has operational consequences.
